  "prompt_text": "This figure is a world map summarizing where atmospheric river frequency appears to increase and what major impacts are reported in different regions. Areas shaded pale pink mark places with increasing AR frequency, concentrated across high southern latitudes and extending into parts of the North Pacific, North Atlantic, and nearby continental margins.\n\nRegional boxes over East Asia, North America, Greenland/Arctic, Europe, South America, South Africa, New Zealand, and the Antarctic Peninsula/West Antarctica compile impact categories using simple icons. The symbols indicate whether studies in each region emphasize frequency, geographic occurrence, precipitation, precipitation extremes, snow or ice melt, and flooding. Colored outlines seem to distinguish stronger or more numerous findings from categories with less support or no clear signal.\n\nTaken together, the map shows that atmospheric rivers are a global phenomenon with regionally different consequences. In Europe and New Zealand, multiple hazard-related categories are highlighted, including precipitation and flooding. Greenland/Arctic and Antarctica prominently feature snow/ice melt, while South Africa emphasizes precipitation-related effects. The figure’s overall message is that increasing atmospheric river activity is not uniform, but where it occurs it is linked to several hydrologic and cryospheric impacts that vary by region.",
